# LeakSanitizer suppressions for Mesa under X11 and Wayland, whichever way CNA reaches the window
# system -- first written for X11 (plans/plan_native_platform_validation.md NPV-0004), then
# extended to EGL. Every entry names a leak that was REPRODUCED WITHOUT ANY CNA CODE, with the
# program and the byte count that proved it; an entry without that evidence does not belong here.
#
# Use:  LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tools/platform/lsan_x11_mesa.supp
#
# Mesa's GLX client (libGLX_mesa) allocates per-display state inside the first
# glXChooseFBConfig on the software (swrast/Xvfb) path and XCloseDisplay does not free it.
# Measured 2026-09-14, Mesa 25.0.7: a 20-line C program doing XOpenDisplay, glXChooseFBConfig,
# glXGetVisualFromFBConfig, XFree, XCloseDisplay -- nothing else -- leaks 100,956 bytes in 449
# allocations per display under Xvfb, which is exactly what each CNA GL test reported. The same
# program leaks nothing against a hardware radeonsi (DRI3) display.
leak:libGLX_mesa.so
#
# Not suppressed, deliberately: the RADV Vulkan ICD leaks 2 x 128 bytes from a pthread_once
# initialiser during vkCreateInstance (reproduced the same way with a CNA-free program; absent with
# only the lavapipe ICD loaded). Its frames belong to a module already unloaded at exit, so no
# suppression can name it without also matching genuine leaks through libvulkan. Sanitizer runs
# of the Vulkan surface tests therefore select lavapipe with
# VK_ICD_FILENAMES=/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/lvp_icd.json, and the RADV allocation is recorded as a
# driver finding instead of being hidden.
#
# Mesa's gallium driver (radeonsi) allocates screen-level state on the first draw of a context
# that only eglTerminate frees. CNA's lazily created default platform is deliberately never
# destroyed (CurrentPlatform.cpp), so a process that draws through a standalone GraphicsDevice
# exits without eglTerminate. Measured 2026-09-24, Mesa 25.0.7 radeonsi (Radeon 780M): a CNA-free
# EGL program that creates a desktop core context, draws one triangle into a framebuffer object and
# exits without eglTerminate leaks exactly 2,112 bytes in 44 allocations -- on the Wayland and on
# the surfaceless EGL platform alike -- which is exactly what every CNA desktop-GL test drawing
# through EGL reported. With eglTerminate, or without the draw, the same program leaks nothing.
leak:libgallium
